":"A good sound professional develops their ears through continuous listening practice."}]},{"heading":"Microphone Knowledge","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Learn the differences between:"},{"type":"h","text":"Dynamic microphones"},{"type":"p","text":"Commonly used for:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Live vocals"},{"text":"Drums"},{"text":"Amplifiers"},{"text":"High-SPL sources"}]},{"type":"h","text":"Condenser microphones"},{"type":"p","text":"Commonly used for:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Studio vocals"},{"text":"Acoustic instruments"},{"text":"Overheads"},{"text":"Detailed recordings"}]},{"type":"h","text":"Ribbon microphones"},{"type":"p","text":"Used in certain studio applications for their distinctive response and character."},{"type":"p","text":"Also understand:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Polar patterns"},{"text":"Cardioid"},{"text":"Supercardioid"},{"text":"Hypercardioid"},{"text":"Omnidirectional"},{"text":"Figure-8"}]}]},{"heading":"Microphone Placement","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Microphone selection alone does not produce good audio."},{"type":"p","text":"Learn:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Distance"},{"text":"Angle"},{"text":"Height"},{"text":"Proximity effect"},{"text":"Off-axis response"},{"text":"Room reflections"},{"text":"Isolation"}]},{"type":"h","text":"Basic recording principle"},{"type":"flow","items":["Sound Source","Room / Acoustics","Microphone Position","Microphone","Preamp","Recording"]},{"type":"p","text":"The room and microphone position can have a major impact on the final recording."}]},{"heading":"Gain Staging","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A Sound Assistant must understand gain structure."},{"type":"flow","items":["Source","Microphone Level","Preamp Gain","Mixer Channel","Bus / Master","Output"]},{"type":"p","text":"Avoid:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Clipping"},{"text":"Excessive noise"},{"text":"Unnecessary gain"},{"text":"Poor signal-to-noise ratio"}]},{